if  what i'm about to say is not eye-opening  i  don't know  what would be hey everyone i'm abby  sharp welcome to abby's kitchen  [Music]  we will be discussing the intersection  between digestive and eating disorders  but first let me tell you about my  sponsor sundance now i have barely left  my house over the past two years  but if anything has gotten me through  this  it's been a good tv series so we  recently got a subscription to sundance  now which is an ad streaming service  created by amc networks for folks like  me who want really solid storytelling  and fresh perspectives they have  original dramas thrillers and my  favorite lots of true crime i mean i  watch an unusual amount of true crime  my hobbyist low-key scared of me as he  should be but they have weekly releases  and hundreds of exclusive shows that you  won't find anywhere else in a variety of  genres that the whole family will love  right now i am totally obsessed with  their original series a discovery of  witches i'm not usually into fantasy but  i'm just so in love with the characters  and the storyline is so strangely  addictive so you can stream sundance now  on all apple or android devices amazon  fire tv google chromecast or roku for  just 4.99 a month with no annoying  commercial interruptions and as a  special gift to my followers you can try  sundance now for free for 30 days by  going to  sundancenow.com and using my promo code  abby sharp and don't forget to come back  here to the comments to let us all know  what shows you are loving right now and  don't forget to pause the screen to look  at the description including a huge  trigger warning for folks with current  or previous experiences with disordered  eating i will be discussing common ed  behaviors so as always feel free to skip  this video if it's not supportive to  your journey and don't forget to  subscribe to this channel if you're not  already ring that little bell and follow  me over on tik tok and instagram okay so  for folks who may be new here  you may not know a lot about my personal  medical history but in short when i was  a teenager i had a pretty severe eating  disorder and almost 20 years later now  i'm still dealing with some of the  potential repercussions  most notably with my ibs and my now sibo  diagnosis which i have been working  through in my series right here now in  my video about my eating disorder i  mentioned that eating disorders and  digestive disorders frequently present  together one study found that of women  diagnosed with an eating disorder 50  met the criteria for ibs and 98  met the criteria for at least one  functional gut disorder and while most  evidence suggests that the eating  disorder often comes first  we are dealing with a very complicated  chicken in the egg situation so what are  the major factors bridging digestive and  eating disorders get ready to learn a  lot first of all let's talk about poor  gut motility so severe caloric  restriction literally starves the vital  organs and in most severe cases  this is why eating disorders can result  in heart failure and even death but  naturally this means the digestive  organs start to atrophy  which can lead to constipation bloating  gas and gastroparesis aka poor gut  motility so research suggests that  bloating is significantly higher in  individuals with eds as is experiences  like nausea acid reflux and prolonged  fullness after a meal and since a lot of  folks with disordered eating histories  are  hyper aware of their body sensations if  they experience bloating after eating  something this can often be interpreted  as feeling fat or gaining weight even  which of course can then hinder their  progress towards recovery so second is  the dysbiosis of the gut so you guys  always hear me say that variety is the  spice of life  and that's not just for feeding yourself  but also for feeding your gut bacteria  as well so limited food variety can  disrupt the balance of bacteria in the  gut and digestive enzyme production  while potentially also exacerbating or  causing food intolerances so for example  if you were to completely remove dairy  from your diet because some influencer  told you dairy is toxic  your body will cease production of the  enzymes needed to digest dairy then  maybe when you slip up or you have a  cheat day and you have some ice cream  you'll likely feel really really really  sick which then reinforces the fear of  this food now quick side note this  enzyme down regulation is reversible  when we actually start to eat normally  again and reintroduce those foods but i  will speak more about that in a moment  but bottom line the result of dysbiosis  is actually more bloating in digestion  gas diarrhea or chronic constipation now  third is excess fiber consumption so  let's get it straight  that it's not uncommon for disordered  eating or eating disorders to stem from  very completely innocent and seemingly  normal  diet or weight loss efforts so it's  quite common for folks trying to eat  healthier or to lose weight to reduce  their consumption of refined carbs or  fats while increasing their consumption  of a lot of low calorie high fiber foods  like vegetables and fruit now consuming  a super load of fodmaps or even just a  lot of fiber-rich foods can cause excess  gas and bloating especially if there's  already dysbiosis or bacterial  overgrowth already present likewise  folks who are consuming a lot of high  fiber snack foods like bars and drinks  may find themselves bloated from the  added inulin fiber now note that there  is nothing unhealthy about inulin fiber  i want to make that very clear  in fact it's a prebiotic so it is  technically very healthy for the gut but  for folks with already compromised guts  it is a pretty aggressive fodmap that is  notorious for causing gas and bloating  so just something to watch out for if  you are already struggling with some of  those symptoms number four is excess air  consumption again it's very common  advice in the weight loss community to  fill your plate with high volume foods  to help suppress your appetite and  therefore reduce your intake of calories  but consuming a lot of air infused foods  like popcorn or rice cakes  chewing a lot of gum  or drinking lots of carbonated drinks to  like suppress your appetite can all  result in swallowing a lot of air and  while air  obviously doesn't have calories it does  add volume to your gut which again gives  the feeling and look of bloat now number  five is other additives in diet products  so sugar is one of the most demonized  ingredients in the wellness or diet  community so a lot of folks who are  trying to lose weight or even just be  healthier we'll swap it out for  something sugar-free but consuming an  excess of sweeteners can cause some  digestive distress most notably a lot of  the sugar alcohols like mannitol  sorbitol and maltitol other emulsifiers  found in low calorie diet snack foods  like carrageenan are more controversial  with some early animal evidence  suggesting an association with ibs and  dysbiosis now i would say that any of  these additives are unlikely to do harm  in moderation but  a lot of folks with eating disorders  tend to eat a small handful of the same  safe foods  every single day and if some of those  foods are chronically irritating the gut  obviously it's going to be hard to ever  get relief now number six is a reliance  on laxatives so laxative use whether  pharmaceuticals supplements like  magnesium or food based like coffee are  not uncommon fixtures in the lives of  folks with eds this may be because of  impaired bowel function from under  nutrition so folks kind of need a  laxative to go  or maybe it's to promote weight loss or  even to suppress appetite in the case of  something like black coffee but laxative  abuse essentially impairs the digestive  system's normal muscle tone resulting in  a lazy gut incapable of normal function  and as you can imagine this just becomes  a vicious cycle now number seven is  related to the brain gut access now this  one is pivotal because we know how  important the intersection of mental  health and digestive health can be so  recent suggests that in patients with  eating disorders specific psychological  gestures like anxiety tend to predict  functional gut disorders like ibs  bloating and constipation now it's  possible that a mental health disorder  is at the core here of both of these  issues and it's also possible that one  is perpetuating the other  so for example folks with eating  disorders often will have anxiety around  eating or eating certain foods that they  don't deem safe which can actually  manifest as physical symptoms to those  foods like bloating constipation and  excess gas  and finally there's the impact of  elimination diets now a lot of the diets  used to treat digestive disorders  are restrictive as  i know because when i started my journey  with my gut i had to be really honest  with myself that doing a full out low  fodmap elimination diet would have  likely triggered me and i know that  because my eating disorder from way back  20 years ago  actually originated with an innocent  elimination diet prescribed by a  naturopathic doctor and then it just  spiraled into a full-blown fear of food  literally all of these things that we've  just talked about happen to me and they  happen to so many others as well who are  just trying to feel better in their  day-to-day so what can you do to treat  your functional gut disorder without  further perpetuating your ed well tip  one unfollow any influencer who promises  to heal your gut with another diet and  just work with a teen who has experience  with functional gut disorders and eating  disorders if a practitioner does not  screen for a disordered eating or  ignores your history while prescribing  another really restrictive diet that's a  really big red flag folks  we cannot heal the gut  until we've addressed the eating  disorder and if this treatment is  happening concurrently it needs to be  handled by a really experienced team i  also recommend recruiting a loved one or  family member to support you and to eat  with you which will make it a lot easier  for you to get through some of the more  challenging points now tip two is to eat  regular meals and snacks at regular  intervals every day aka mechanical  eating so this is usually around three  meals and two to three snacks a day and  it is necessary for a lot of people to  regain hunger cues and to regulate  normal motility patterns intuitive  eating is just not really possible for  folks with active eating disorders so  keep in mind that it's normal for this  to feel very unintuitive at first now  tip number three is to work towards the  greatest variety of foods possible again  i acknowledge how scary this is  definitely going to feel but it is  imperative that we begin to expose  ourselves to these foods to help reduce  the food fears but also to help improve  bacterial diversity to get those  critters in your gut working in your  favor now tip number four is to consider  bowel training so i spoke to my  colleague and registered dietitian dasha  egg nulik who i will link to below  she told me that just like our bicep  muscles grow big when we do our bicep  curls weisenberg biclaps classic newton  bison burger bicentennial man our  muscles need training to go to the  bathroom too so she recommends sitting  on the toilet with your legs propped up  to create some pressure from your thighs  into the sides of your abdomen so she  suggests doing this first thing in the  morning for 10 minutes and then again  about 30 minutes after lunch and dinner  for 10 minutes every day  for about two months if you poop while  you're on the toilet great  toilet  sitting on the toilet if you don't  that's all good too think of this as gym  time for your bowel now tip number five  is to focus on self-care and stress  management a stressed mind means a  stressed gut so we need to really  address some of the root emotions before  we can really change any of the  problematic coping strategies tip 6  consider the right supplements ideally  here you want to work with a trained  expert and complete any relevant tests  to determine your unique supplement  needs so for example i did a gi 360  stool test and a sibo breath test myself  to get some hard data on which  supplements were best for me honestly  the supplement world is not perfect and  there's a lot of bs to get through but  for folks with disordered eating  tendencies i do prefer to add  supplements over taking other foods away  so depending on your symptoms this could  mean unique strains of probiotics  digestive enzymes supplemental acids  bile a fiber supplement like partially  hydrolyzed guar gum etc especially if  you're trying to wean off more  aggressive laxatives and just improve  your natural gut motility a really good  fiber regimen may be  key and then finally tip seven  manage symptoms without restriction  listen there are going to be hard days  emotionally and physically and i'm gonna  speak more to what to expect in just a  moment but try to manage the symptoms as  they come so this may be like a warm  bath or a gentle walk some yoga poses a  hot water bottle on your tummy or a cup  of peppermint tea when you feel  digestive symptoms emerge but remember  this too  shall pass  shall not  pass  but of course speaking of symptoms what  should you expect when you're trying to  heal your gut after or during an eating  disorder well  if we're being honest you probably will  feel worse before you feel better  emotionally and also physically if  you're practicing mechanical eating  you're probably going to feel  uncomfortably full between meals and  this can be very triggering for you  you'll also likely feel bloated gassy  puffy edemic and have other digestive  symptoms as your body just starts to  play catch-up on all of the enzyme  production shifts in fluids and  electrolytes and your gut's bacterial  profile recalibrates you may not be able  to tolerate every single food without  some kind of symptom at least at first  but according to all of my colleagues  who i asked and also the literature  symptoms will significantly improve  virtually in every case when you just  start eating enough calories enough  variety and you're able to manage your  fear and stress of eating remember this  is a long game but it is so  worth it for long-term food freedom now  i hope this video was helpful for you  guys and that you now have some great  tools in your toolbox for relief when  you're struggling with your digestion  and your relationship with food a big  reminder to of course seek out the  support of a trained dietitian some of  which i'm going to link to below and if  you like this video be sure to give it  the thumbs up leave me a comment below  if you have any other major questions  any other topics you want me to cover  subscribe to the channel and i'll see  you next time on abby's kitchen bye  [Music]

